WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.011 --> 00:00:03.678
(soft, mellow guitar music)

00:00:05.562 --> 00:00:07.235
- [Karl] When you're
on the trail that long,

00:00:07.235 --> 00:00:09.425
you don't really think about
how long is it gonna take me

00:00:09.425 --> 00:00:11.786
to get to somewhere or
how fast am I going,

00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:14.309
you're thinking about,
don't get yourself hurt.

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:18.142
(lively, bouncy guitar music)

00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:22.228
My name is Karl Meltzer, and
I am an ultra-marathoner.

00:00:22.228 --> 00:00:23.547
- We did 36 miles today--

00:00:23.547 --> 00:00:24.942
- Yeah, it was a pretty weak day, but--

00:00:24.942 --> 00:00:27.557
No one's ever been this far in 10 days.

00:00:27.557 --> 00:00:29.894
I just recently broke the
Appalachian Trail record

00:00:29.894 --> 00:00:33.536
from Maine to Georgia, it's 2,189 miles,

00:00:33.536 --> 00:00:35.914
and I completed that in 45 days,

00:00:35.914 --> 00:00:39.456
22 hours and 38 minutes,
beating the previous record

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:42.073
by just a little under 10 hours.

00:00:42.073 --> 00:00:43.494
Total mileage average per day

00:00:43.494 --> 00:00:46.654
for that 46 days would be 47.6.

00:00:46.654 --> 00:00:50.821
The AT is not a walk in the
park; it's a hard trail.

00:00:55.582 --> 00:00:56.593
You really have to have a team;

00:00:56.593 --> 00:00:59.572
it's not just the one guy runnin'.

00:00:59.572 --> 00:01:03.068
It's really the crew who, if
they're not perfect for you,

00:01:03.068 --> 00:01:05.536
then it really won't be successful.

00:01:05.536 --> 00:01:07.377
(daunting orchestral music)

00:01:07.377 --> 00:01:10.164
I think the Appalachian Trail,
not only is it very popular

00:01:10.164 --> 00:01:12.495
and iconic, but it's also very difficult.

00:01:12.495 --> 00:01:14.476
Now it's all down hill and easy.

00:01:14.476 --> 00:01:16.876
I am concentrating on my
foot placement all the time,

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:19.586
so two hours goes by like
nothin', just because I'm zoned,

00:01:19.586 --> 00:01:21.009
I'm zoned out.

00:01:21.009 --> 00:01:23.495
I slammed my head into the
ground a number of times

00:01:23.495 --> 00:01:26.377
and it beats you down,
you have to expect that,

00:01:26.377 --> 00:01:27.873
but you can beat it too as long as you

00:01:27.873 --> 00:01:30.371
just keep persevering and
fighting through the issues

00:01:30.371 --> 00:01:32.538
that you're going to have.

00:01:33.636 --> 00:01:35.839
The last day, I ran 85 miles.

00:01:35.839 --> 00:01:37.651
My body was so amped and ready to be done

00:01:37.651 --> 00:01:40.258
that it didn't matter
really how far it was.

00:01:40.258 --> 00:01:42.801
I felt the same at mile
80 as I did at mile 10.

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:45.341
You're just numb at that point really.

00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:46.630
This is where all the fun is.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:47.939
It's important for people
to know that the AT

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:50.758
is a special place and
it should be protected.

00:01:50.758 --> 00:01:52.548
It's a trail for one who wants to gain

00:01:52.548 --> 00:01:54.950
fellowship with nature,
no matter whether you're

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.761
running it, whether you're walking it,

00:01:56.761 --> 00:01:58.796
whether you're taking 10 years to do it,

00:01:58.796 --> 00:02:02.847
when you walk that trail, you
have a vibe, and you feel it.

00:02:02.847 --> 00:02:05.902
Every year someone else
gets a little bit faster.

00:02:05.902 --> 00:02:07.852
Someone will break my record for sure.

00:02:07.852 --> 00:02:09.372
It might come this year, I don't know.

00:02:09.372 --> 00:02:10.742
I'm still gonna be
happy because I gave it

00:02:10.742 --> 00:02:11.575
everything I had.

00:02:11.575 --> 00:02:13.284
I think that's what's important.

00:02:13.284 --> 00:02:16.534
(lively, bouncy music)

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:22.838
(chiming reverberation)

